# Adjudicated punused findings for vibekit.
#
# The go-ci punused step scans one directory at a time (a whole-module run
# silently under-reports) and checks every finding line against this file.
# A full-line "#" comment carries the reason; every other non-empty line is
# ONE literal substring matched against the finding line. Never put
# file:line:col in a pattern — line numbers shift and the entry goes stale
# (the gate then warns "matches nothing").
#
# Nothing below is dead code. Real dead code is deleted or wired up, never
# whitelisted. Read the codes precisely: EU1002 = punused/gopls found no
# reference at all; EU1001 = every reference is in a _test.go file. Neither
# code can see a call made through an interface, through reflection
# (encoding/json), from another language, or from a _test.go DECLARATION
# (punused never inspects those, though references from them do count).
#
# One entry may report as "matches nothing" on a given run: punused caps a
# single invocation at a 30s context deadline and drops the rest of the
# shard silently (the masked error reads "file already closed", which the
# gate filters), and internal/api is over that budget. The policy.go entry
# is the one that flickers. Entries here are the union of a complete
# per-FILE scan, so this file stays a superset either way.

# --- 1. Cross-language wire contract: enum members the TypeScript client
# consumes. cmd/wire-codegen walks the Go AST (wiregen) and emits
# static-src/wire/types.gen.ts; each value below is a member of a union in
# that generated file, and the generated decoder rejects any value outside
# its union. Deleting the Go constant silently shrinks the contract, so a
# legitimate server value would fail client-side decode. Verified by
# grepping the VALUE, not the Go identifier:
#   "other"          -> types.gen.ts ToolKind union   (+ static-src/tool-schema.ts OTHER profile)
#   "refusal"        -> types.gen.ts StopReason union (+ static-src/refusal.ts)
#   "stream_timeout" -> types.gen.ts ErrorCode union
#   "spawn_failed"   -> types.gen.ts ErrorCode union
constant ToolKindOther
constant StopReasonRefusal
constant ErrCodeStreamTimeout
constant ErrCodeSpawnFailed

# --- 2. json-tagged fields on wiregen-registered structs. The struct is
# registered in cmd/wire-codegen, so the field is part of the generated TS
# interface AND its validating decoder; encoding/json populates it by
# reflection, which no reference search can see. Several are REQUIRED in the
# generated decoder, so deleting the Go field breaks the client outright:
#   PlanEntry.Priority        -> types.gen.ts PlanEntry.priority, decoders.gen.ts reqStr($.plan_entry)
#   Usage.MeteringItems       -> types.gen.ts Usage.metering_items, decodeArray($.usage.metering_items)
#   MeteringItem.UnitSingular -> reqStr($.metering_item)
#   MeteringItem.UnitPlural   -> reqStr($.metering_item)
#   MeteringItem.Value        -> types.gen.ts MeteringItem.value
#   CommandsUpdatedPayload.Prompts -> types.gen.ts CommandsUpdatedPayload.prompts
field Priority is
field MeteringItems is
field UnitSingular is
field UnitPlural is
field Value is
field Prompts is

# Whole-file entries: every exported struct in these two files is a
# wiregen-registered wire type (ElicitationPropertySchema,
# ElicitationRequestSchema / PolicyRuleCore, PolicyRule, PolicyView,
# PolicyExplainResult, PolicyErrorItem), so every field is generated into
# types.gen.ts + decoders.gen.ts and filled by encoding/json. The
# elicitation schemas are pass-through JSON-Schema: vibekit forwards the
# MCP server's form definition to the client verbatim and never inspects a
# property, which is exactly why no Go code reads these fields.
internal/api/elicitation.go
internal/api/policy.go

# --- 3. Inbound envelopes decoded by encoding/json, deliberately not read.
# internal/api/commands.go holds the client -> server command payloads; the
# browser sends every field and json.Unmarshal fills it. Attachment.Name is
# the only unread one: internal/command/prompt_attachments.go re-derives the
# display name with filepath.Base(att.Path) rather than trusting a
# client-supplied string that reaches log lines and agent-visible text.
# Deleting it would erase the record of what the client actually sends.
internal/api/commands.go
# RPCResponse.JSONRPC is the inbound half of the ACP JSON-RPC envelope
# (the outbound RPCRequest/RPCNotification/RPCResponseOut copies ARE set in
# internal/bridge/bridge_rpc.go). The reader ignores the version field, but
# json.Unmarshal populates it and the bridge tests build realistic
# envelopes with it.
field JSONRPC is

# --- 4. Inbound ACP wire shapes (internal/translate/wire.go). Every struct
# in that file is a json.Unmarshal target for a KAS session update. Two
# fields are decoded and deliberately unread: _meta.kiro.kind (the
# "agent-subtask" discriminator — the translator keys off agentSubtaskId
# instead) and _meta.kiro.refusal.explanation (duplicates the chunk text,
# so only Category/RecommendedModel flow into api.RefusalInfo). Both
# document the wire contract at the only place it is expressed in code.
internal/translate/wire.go

# --- 5. fmt.Stringer implementations on typed identifiers. fmt and slog
# discover String() by reflection at %s/%v sites, so no reference exists to
# find. All four are string-kind types whose String() returns the
# underlying string, giving the ID family one uniform conversion affordance
# (api.ChatID/SessionID/ModelID, mcp.ServerID).
method (ChatID).String
method (SessionID).String
method (ModelID).String
method (ServerID).String

# --- 6. error-interface machinery. Error() is called by whatever formats
# the error; Unwrap() is what makes errors.Is/As walk the chain. Call
# sites: internal/command/prompt.go errors.AsType[*api.TransportError],
# and internal/forges/cliexec constructs &CmdError{Err: ...} as an error.
method (*TransportError).Unwrap
method (*CmdError).Error
method (*CmdError).Unwrap

# --- 7. container/heap.Interface method sets. The stdlib calls Len/Less/
# Swap/Push/Pop through the interface: internal/buffer/lines.go uses
# heap.Pop/heap.Fix/heap.Push on &state.h (fileHeap) and internal/dedup
# uses heap.Init/heap.Push/heap.Pop on &c.evictHeap (evictionHeap).
# Removing any member breaks the interface at compile time.
method (fileHeap).
method (*fileHeap).
method (evictionHeap).
method (*evictionHeap).

# --- 8. http.Handler. internal/hub/hub.go registers the command dispatcher
# with mux.Handle("/api/command", h.dispatcher), so net/http invokes
# ServeHTTP through the interface.
method (*Dispatcher).ServeHTTP

# --- 9. io.Writer implementations wired into os/exec. Each buffer is
# assigned to a subprocess's Stdout/Stderr, so os/exec calls Write:
#   ShellCappedBuffer -> internal/command/shell.go   shellProc.Stdout/Stderr
#   hookCappedBuffer  -> internal/hub/hooks.go       proc.Stdout/Stderr
#   RingBuffer        -> internal/mcp/prewarm        cmd.Stdout/Stderr
#   cappedBuffer      -> internal/server/cli.go      cmd.Stdout
method (*ShellCappedBuffer).Write
method (*hookCappedBuffer).Write
method (*RingBuffer).Write
method (*cappedBuffer).Write

# --- 10. json.Unmarshaler. encoding/json calls (*mcp.Server).UnmarshalJSON
# whenever the servers file is decoded; it is the boundary that rejects an
# unknown transport value. Deleting it would silently accept one.
method (*Server).UnmarshalJSON

# --- 11. internal/testsupport fakes. This package exists so sibling
# packages' TEST binaries share one implementation, so EU1001 is its
# design, not a defect. The EU1002 members are interface-satisfaction
# stubs pinned by the compile-time assertions in these files:
#   var _ api.ChatStore  = NopChatStore{}                (chatstore.go)
#   var _ api.ChatStore  = (*RecordingChatStore)(nil)    (chatstore.go)
#   var _ api.ChatStore  = (*InMemoryChatStore)(nil)     (inmem_chatstore.go)
#   var _ api.Broadcaster = (*CaptureBroadcaster)(nil)   (broadcaster.go)
# Consumers: internal/hub (fakeChatStore = testsupport.RecordingChatStore),
# internal/command and internal/translate (NewInMemoryChatStore).
internal/testsupport/chatstore.go
internal/testsupport/inmem_chatstore.go
internal/testsupport/broadcaster.go

# --- 12. internal/testsupport shared contract suites. Each is a t.Run
# battery invoked from another package's _test.go: internal/chat and
# internal/hub (ChatStoreContractTest), internal/mcp and internal/hub
# (MCPConfigContractTest), internal/testsupport's own bridge_test.go
# (ACPBridgeContractTest, post-Start smoke), internal/bridge and
# internal/hub (ACPBridgePreStartContractTest, pre-Start lifecycle).
internal/testsupport/chatstore_contract.go
internal/testsupport/mcpconfig_contract.go
internal/testsupport/bridge_contract.go
internal/testsupport/bridge_prestart_contract.go

# --- 13. Test-isolation seam that prevents writing the developer's own
# $HOME. internal/mcp's Store renders KAS's config file, whose default path
# resolves under KiroHome() — i.e. ~/.kiro/settings/mcp.json. Before this
# option existed, the package's own tests constructed a store and OVERWROTE
# the developer's real file (observed, not hypothetical). WithKASConfigPath
# is how every test points that write at t.TempDir(). It is a functional
# option rather than a package var on purpose: a global override has to be
# remembered at each call site, while an argument is visible in the
# construction itself. The default is pinned by
# TestNew_DefaultKASPathIsUnderKiroHome, so production cannot silently
# drift to the test path either.
function WithKASConfigPath

# --- 14. Test-isolation seam that prevents writing the developer's own
# $HOME, part two. SetKiroHomeForTest installs a kiro-home RESOLVER (not
# just a cached value: KiroHome() only consults the cache when a resolver
# is present, so seeding the cache alone silently did nothing and the
# "override" fell through to os.UserHomeDir()). Four packages' tests use
# it (composition, mcp, steering x2) to point ~/.kiro writes at
# t.TempDir(); without it they overwrite the developer's real files.
function SetKiroHomeForTest

# --- 15. Cross-package test synchronisation. The purge scheduler runs a
# goroutine; internal/chat's store tests (a DIFFERENT package from
# archive, so no access to the unexported done field) wait on Done() to
# assert the drain instead of sleeping. archive's own tests read .done
# directly.
method (*PurgeScheduler).Done

